<DocsLayout
  title="Working with local files — EweserDB docs"
  description="Open a vault folder so Ewe Note notes stay real Markdown files on your computer, on one machine or on several."
  heading="Working with local files"
  standfirst="Files are a projection of the vault, not the vault itself. Any computer can hold them, and a computer that does not still gets every note."
>
  <h2>Open a vault folder</h2>
  <p>
    In Ewe Note, go to <strong>Settings</strong> and choose
    <strong>Open vault folder</strong>. Pick a folder of Markdown files — an
    Obsidian vault works as-is. The files become ordinary notes, and the browser
    keeps a writable link to the originals, so edits in Ewe Note write back to
    the same files on disk.
  </p>
  <p>
    The structure comes with them: subfolders become folders, frontmatter, tags
    and <code>[[wiki links]]</code> are preserved, and
    <code>.git</code>, <code>.obsidian</code>, <code>.trash</code> and
    <code>node_modules</code> are skipped.
  </p>
  <p>
    Signed in, those notes also sync like any other note. So the machine with
    the folder open has real files <em>and</em> sync; your phone has the same notes
    with no files involved.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-callout">
    <p>
      Writable folder access needs a browser that supports the File System
      Access API — Chrome, Edge or another Chromium browser on the desktop.
      Elsewhere you can still import a folder, but edits will not write back to
      disk.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Files on more than one computer</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing limits files to one machine. Open the same vault's folder on a
    second computer and both hold real files, both sync through the same vault.
    They do not need the same folder path, or the same folder name.
  </p>
  <p>
    For an always-on setup that does not depend on a browser tab being open, run
    the sync daemon from the repository instead:
  </p>
  <pre><code>npx tsx packages/ewe-note/src/cli/vault-sync.ts \
  --vault /path/to/vault \
  --room &lt;room-id&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>
    It watches the folder and the vault in both directions, and debounces rapid
    edits. Run it on each machine that should keep files. It is a command-line
    tool from the <a href={DOCS_URLS.github} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
      >EweserDB repository</a
    >, not a packaged app, so it suits a desktop you leave running.
  </p>

  <h2>When the same note changed in both places</h2>
  <p>
    The vault version wins and the other version is written next to it as
    <code>Note (conflict 2026-01-01T12-00-00-000Z).md</code>, then added to the
    vault too. Nothing is overwritten and nothing is thrown away; you merge the
    two by hand and delete the copy.
  </p>

  <h2>Attachments</h2>
  <p>
    Images and other attachments sync through object storage rather than by
    copying local paths, because a path on one computer means nothing on
    another. A device materialises the file when it needs it.
  </p>

  <p class="docs-next">
    Next: <a href="/docs/secure-vaults">Secure vaults</a>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
